.SS cbor::category
Gives the one cbor::error_category of cbor::error.
Each call gives the same object.
Its name is \f[CR]\(dqcbor\(dq\f[R].
A \f[CR]std::error_code\f[R] made from a \f[CR]cbor::error\f[R] holds
this category.
.SS cbor::error_category
The \f[CR]std::error_category\f[R] of cbor::error.
A program reaches it through cbor::category().
\f[CR]name()\f[R] gives \f[CR]\(dqcbor\(dq\f[R].
\f[CR]message\f[R] gives the text of the error, for example
\f[CR]\(dqkey not found\(dq\f[R], and
\f[CR]\(dqunknown cbor error\(dq\f[R] for a value that is no
\f[CR]cbor::error\f[R].
.PP
\f[CR]default_error_condition\f[R] maps four errors to a
\f[CR]std::errc\f[R]: \f[CR]no_buffer_space\f[R],
\f[CR]value_too_large\f[R], \f[CR]not_enough_memory\f[R] and
\f[CR]io_error\f[R], each to the \f[CR]std::errc\f[R] of the same name.
Every other error maps to a condition of this category.
So only these four compare equal to a \f[CR]std::errc\f[R].
.SS cbor::make_error_code
Makes a \f[CR]std::error_code\f[R] with the value of \f[CR]e\f[R] and
the cbor::category() of cbor::error.
The constructor of \f[CR]std::error_code\f[R] from a
\f[CR]cbor::error\f[R] calls it by argument\-dependent lookup.
So a program seldom calls it by name.
.SS std::is_error_code_enum<cbor::error>
Marks cbor::error as an error code enum.
So a \f[CR]cbor::error\f[R] converts to a \f[CR]std::error_code\f[R]
with no cast, through cbor::make_error_code().
A \f[CR]std::error_code\f[R] also compares equal to a
\f[CR]cbor::error\f[R] with \f[CR]==\f[R].
A \f[CR]cbor::error\f[R] and a \f[CR]std::errc\f[R] do not compare
directly: that does not compile.
Put a \f[CR]std::error_code\f[R] on the left.
.SS cbor::limits
Gives the limits of the build.
The macro \f[CR]CBOR_STRING_LENGTH_DEFAULT\f[R] sets
\f[CR]string_length\f[R], and the macro
\f[CR]CBOR_CONTAINER_ELEMENTS_DEFAULT\f[R] sets
\f[CR]container_elements\f[R].
A macro that the build does not set gets its default,
\f[CR]SIZE_MAX\f[R].
No call reads a limit at run time.
A limit at \f[CR]SIZE_MAX\f[R] is not set, and its check is not in the
program.
A limit counts what a call reads or decodes.
An item that a call only skips does not count.
Every translation unit of one program uses the same values.
.PP
Nesting has no fixed limit.
A reader that recurses stops where the stack of its thread ends: a call
that comes within 16 KiB of the end (32 KiB on Windows or with a
sanitizer) returns \f[CR]nesting_depth_exceeded\f[R].
Each thread reads the bounds of its own stack once, at its first check:
from the system on Linux (glibc and musl), macOS and Windows, and from
the linker on WebAssembly without threads and on picolibc.
Another target does not compile.
A frame outside these bounds, such as the stack of a fiber or of a
signal handler, is not checked.
The first check of a thread does not run in a signal handler.
On Linux, a change of \f[CR]RLIMIT_STACK\f[R] after the first check, and
a mapping that a program places with \f[CR]MAP_FIXED\f[R] below the
stack of the main thread, are outside the bound.
On picolibc, a stack smaller than the reserve refuses every nested item.
cbor::item_size(), cbor::sequence_reader and a path of child segments do
not recurse and read any depth.
A deep value of a user type (\f[CR]structs\f[R], \f[CR]schema\f[R]) is
destroyed and copied by its own recursion: a thread with a smaller stack
than the one that decoded it can overflow there.
.PP
